COMMAND "Add a bp CLI Verb" DESCRIPTION "Give a plugin a working `bp <noun> <verb>` by appending one data map to its cli_commands/0 list. Zero Go: the manifest-driven CLI grows the verb for free." LAST_UPDATED "16-07-2026-00-00-00" DOMAIN "barkpark" TAGS "cli", "plugin", "verb" CONCEPT "cli-verb" VARIANT "read-list" VARIABLES
  VARIABLE 1 "Noun" TITLE "Plugin noun the verb hangs off" DESCRIPTION "The existing noun in the plugin's cli_commands/0 list this verb extends. Lowercase single word — the four casings coincide." EXAMPLES "ticket"
  VARIABLE 2 "Verb" TITLE "The new verb" DESCRIPTION "The verb `bp <noun> <verb>` will answer to. Lowercase single word — the four casings coincide." EXAMPLES "stats", "waiting", "unassigned"
  VARIABLE 3 "Summary" TITLE "One-line operator summary (manifest + --help text)" DESCRIPTION "Shown in `bp <noun> --help` and the capabilities manifest. Opaque literal, no casing transform." EXAMPLES "Operator dashboard: open / answered / closed counts, oldest-waiting age."
  VARIABLE 4 "PathTemplate" TITLE "The HTTP route the verb calls (GET)" DESCRIPTION "The path_template the generic HTTP builder calls. Must be a route the API actually serves. Opaque literal in URL position ({{.path_template}} snake spelling)." EXAMPLES "/v1/tickets/stats"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Why this command is a ONE-LINE append (the architecture already did the work)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There is NO handler key to write. `bp` is manifest-driven: the Go binary
# (internal/cli) has zero per-command code. It reads GET /v1/capabilities, walks
# the noun/verb tree (tree.Lookup), and builds the HTTP call GENERICALLY from the
# command map's own fields — `internal/cli/cli.go:9` composes the request from
# `http.path_template` via `manifest.BuildURL`, and `internal/cli/run.go:130`
# takes the method straight from `cmd.HTTP.Method`. No switch, no registration,
# no recompile.
#
# The round-trip, end to end:
#
#     cli_commands/0 list entry            (this append — the ONLY edit)
#        │  Plugins.Tickets.cli_commands/0 delegates to Tickets.CLI.commands/0
#        ▼
#     GET /v1/capabilities                 (CapabilitiesController folds every
#        │                                   plugin's maps in, stamping
#        │                                   source: "plugin:tickets" provenance
#        │                                   from the noun)
#        ▼
#     bp <noun> <verb>                     (tree.Lookup finds the map; the
#                                           generic HTTP builder calls
#                                           http.method + http.path_template)
#
# So: append the map here, and `bp ticket <verb>` works on the next capabilities
# fetch. No Go file is touched by this command — that is the whole point.
#
# Exemplar payload: the `ticket.inbox` map, api/lib/barkpark/plugins/tickets/cli.ex:87-104
# — the canonical read-list verb shape (id / noun / verb / summary /
# http{method,path_template} / auth_tier / args / flags / writes / batch /
# paginated / dry_run / default_output / scoped_prefix). This command templates
# that exact archetype: a read-tier GET list verb with a `limit` flag. For a
# WRITING verb, flip `method`/`writes`/`auth_tier`/`default_output` by hand — the
# map SHAPE is identical, only these four values change.
#
# Optional cosmetic polish (NOT part of this command, never a MARK): if the new
# verb returns a single wrapped object and you want its fields rendered as
# key/value lines instead of a one-row table, add the envelope key to
# `singleObjectEnvelopeKeys` at internal/cli/table.go:96. That is a Go rendering
# nicety only; the verb is fully functional without it.

# Token spellings-as-transforms (D3d / A1). A verb is a single lowercase word, so
# the four canonical casings coincide — declared for grammar completeness:
#   {{.Verb}}   → Stats     (Pascal)
#   {{.verb}}   → stats     (kebab == camel == snake, lowercase)

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OP — append the new verb map to the ticket noun's command list (A3)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The list `ticket_commands/0` ends with the `ticket.close` map, whose closing
# `}` carries NO trailing comma before the `]` (verified: cli.ex:149-166). Under
# A3 you cannot bare-INSERT a second map after it — two adjacent `%{…} %{…}` terms
# are a syntax error. The append is therefore a FENCED REPLACE of that last
# element: re-emit `ticket.close` verbatim, add the separating comma, then the new
# map. The `ticket.close` target is unique (`grep -c 'id: "ticket.close"'` = 1).
#
# A4/A5 guard: `ticket.close` survives as a substring of the replacement, so
# idempotency rests ENTIRELY on the ASLONG guard — a payload-derived, ASCII,
# per-verb substring of what the op writes (the new map's `id:` line — chosen over
# the mark line because it ALSO refuses a verb that already exists by hand). No
# `}}` sits adjacent to a literal `}`. On a re-run with the same VARIABLEs the
# guard is already present and the whole op is skipped (no duplicate verb). The
# payload's comment line plants the per-verb mark cli-verb-{{.noun}}-{{.verb}}:
# first run anchors on the `ticket.close` structural target and plants it; every
# later run (and remove) anchors at that mark.

IN "api/lib/barkpark/plugins/tickets/cli.ex"
### Append {{.noun}}.{{.verb}} to ticket_commands/0
REPLACE
::: target :::
      %{
        id: "ticket.close",
        noun: "ticket",
        verb: "close",
        summary: "Close a ticket (status=closed, done).",
        http: %{method: "POST", path_template: "/v1/tickets/:id/close"},
        auth_tier: "read",
        args: [
          %{name: "id", required: true, type: "string", summary: "Ticket id to close."}
        ],
        flags: [],
        writes: true,
        batch: false,
        paginated: false,
        dry_run: false,
        default_output: "minimal",
        scoped_prefix: nil
      }
::: target :::
WITH
::: payload :::
      %{
        id: "ticket.close",
        noun: "ticket",
        verb: "close",
        summary: "Close a ticket (status=closed, done).",
        http: %{method: "POST", path_template: "/v1/tickets/:id/close"},
        auth_tier: "read",
        args: [
          %{name: "id", required: true, type: "string", summary: "Ticket id to close."}
        ],
        flags: [],
        writes: true,
        batch: false,
        paginated: false,
        dry_run: false,
        default_output: "minimal",
        scoped_prefix: nil
      },
      # scaffy:add-cli-verb {{.noun}}.{{.verb}} MARK:cli-verb-{{.noun}}-{{.verb}}
      %{
        id: "{{.noun}}.{{.verb}}",
        noun: "{{.noun}}",
        verb: "{{.verb}}",
        summary: "{{.summary}}",
        http: %{method: "GET", path_template: "{{.path_template}}"},
        auth_tier: "read",
        args: [],
        flags: [
          %{name: "limit", type: "int", summary: "Max rows to return.", default: 50}
        ],
        writes: false,
        batch: false,
        paginated: true,
        dry_run: false,
        default_output: "table",
        scoped_prefix: nil
      }
::: payload :::
MARK "cli-verb-{{.noun}}-{{.verb}}"
ASLONG FILE DONT CONTAIN "id: \"{{.noun}}.{{.verb}}\""

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# POSTCONDITIONS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ASSERT FILE: the new map is present (id + route). Both strings are verbatim
# substrings the op writes, guard-consistent with the ASLONG above (A4).
ASSERT FILE "api/lib/barkpark/plugins/tickets/cli.ex" CONTAINS "id: \"{{.noun}}.{{.verb}}\""
ASSERT FILE "api/lib/barkpark/plugins/tickets/cli.ex" CONTAINS "path_template: \"{{.path_template}}\""

# ASSERT CMD: the plugin's own CLI-manifest test compiles the map and asserts the
# frozen cli_command() shape. Every Elixir gate is TIER ci (A2) — never run in the
# apply worktree, only on CI where the BEAM toolchain and DB are provisioned.
ASSERT CMD "cd api && mix test test/barkpark/plugins/tickets/cli_test.exs" TIER ci
